  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$27.81 billion** of Chapter 10 goods in 2025, up **14.1%** from 2024. **Corn (heading 1005) is 68%** of that, **wheat (1001) 23%**, **rice (1006) 7%** and **grain sorghum (1007) 2%**. Mexico and Japan buy **43%** of the chapter. South Korea's purchases rose **83.5%**, the EU's **142%**, and Viet Nam's more than quadrupled, while China's collapsed **95%** to $109 million.\n\nTariffs are close to invisible for most large buyers. Mexico, Canada, South Korea and Colombia take U.S. grain duty-free under U.S. trade agreements, and Japan lets feed corn and sorghum in free. Japan's wheat comes through a state-run quota with a government mark-up, and its rice through a small quota. What usually decides sales is the size of the crop, price, biotech approvals and quotas, not a tariff. China is the exception: it still adds an extra 10% to its normal rate, after lifting its 15% tariffs on U.S. corn and wheat on November 10, 2025.",
