  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Corn and wheat are 91% of this chapter: corn (1005) 68% and wheat (1001) 23%. Rice (6.8%) and grain sorghum (1.9%) are the next largest.",
    "Mexico, Canada, South Korea and Colombia take U.S. grain duty-free under free-trade agreements, and Japan admits feed corn and sorghum free; wheat and rice there move through quotas.",
    "China is the outlier. Its purchases of this chapter fell 95% in 2025, to $109 million, while South Korea (+83.5%), the EU (+142%) and Viet Nam (more than four times higher) bought far more.",
    "The U.S. side is routine: a Schedule B number, official FGIS inspection and weighing, a phytosanitary certificate when the buyer wants one, and an AES filing. No export licence applies in the ordinary case."
  ],
